 *******************************************************************************
 **                                                                           **
 **           Bethe Salpeter equation (BSE) for excitation energies           **
 **               solved without Tamm-Dancoff approximation (TDA)             **
 **                                                                           **
 *******************************************************************************
  
 BSE| No cutoff given for occupied orbitals
 BSE| No cutoff given for empty orbitals
 BSE| First occupied index                                                     1
 BSE| Last empty index (not MO index!)                                      1078
 BSE| Energy of first occupied index [eV]                               -307.503
 BSE| Energy of last empty index [eV]                                    750.847
 BSE| Energy difference of first occupied index to HOMO [eV]             299.519
 BSE| Energy difference of last empty index to LUMO [eV]                 750.351
 BSE| Number of GW-corrected occupied MOs                                     48
 BSE| Number of GW-corrected empty MOs                                      1078
 BSE|
 BSE| No truncation of BSE matrices applied
 BSE|
 BSE| Total peak memory estimate from BSE [GB]                         1.995E+02
 BSE| Peak memory estimate per MPI rank from BSE [GB]                      2.078
 BSE|
 BSE| Diagonalizing aux. matrix with size of A. This will take around 2.E+02 s.
 BSE| Diagonalizing C. This will take around 2.E+02 s.
 BSE|
 BSE|
 BSE| **************************************************************************
 BSE| *      Full ("ABBA") Bethe Salpeter equation (BSE) (i.e. without TDA)    *
 BSE| **************************************************************************
 BSE|
 BSE| The excitations are calculated by diagonalizing the BSE without the TDA:
 BSE|
 BSE|                |A B| |X^n|       |1  0| |X^n|
 BSE|                |B A| |Y^n| = Ω^n |0 -1| |Y^n|
 BSE|
 BSE| i.e. in index notation:
 BSE|
 BSE|   sum_jb ( A_ia,jb   X_jb^n + B_ia,jb   Y_jb^n ) = Ω^n X_ia^n
 BSE| - sum_jb ( B_ia,jb   X_jb^n + A_ia,jb   Y_jb^n ) = Ω^n Y_ia^n
 BSE|
 BSE|
 BSE| i,j:       occupied molecular orbitals, i.e. state in          [   1,  48]
 BSE| a,b:       unoccupied molecular orbitals, i.e. state in        [  49,1126]
 BSE| n:         Excitation index
 BSE|
 BSE| A_ia,jb = (ε_a-ε_i) δ_ij δ_ab + α * v_ia,jb - W_ij,ab
 BSE| B_ia,jb = α * v_ia,jb - W_ib,aj
 BSE|
 BSE| prelim Ref.: Eqs. (24-27),(30),(35)
 BSE| in PRB 92,045209 (2015); http://dx.doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.92.045209 .
 BSE|
 BSE| The BSE is solved for Ω^n and X_ia^n as a hermitian problem, e.g. Eq.(42)
 BSE| in PRB 92,045209 (2015); http://dx.doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.92.045209 .
 BSE|
 BSE| ε_...:                 GW quasiparticle energy
 BSE| δ_...:                 Kronecker delta
 BSE| α:                     spin-dependent factor
 BSE| v_...:                 Electron-hole exchange interaction
 BSE| W_... = 1/ϵ v_...:     Direct interaction screened by 
 BSE|                        dielectric function ϵ(ω=0)
 BSE|
 BSE|
 BSE| The spin-dependent factor is for the requested Singlet is α = 2.0
 BSE|
 BSE| Excitation energies from solving the BSE without the TDA:
 BSE|
 BSE|     Excitation n   Spin Config       TDA/ABBA   Excitation energy Ω^n (eV)
 BSE|                1       Singlet         -ABBA-                       4.2549
 BSE|                2       Singlet         -ABBA-                       4.4817
 BSE|                3       Singlet         -ABBA-                       5.0230
 BSE|                4       Singlet         -ABBA-                       5.0538
 BSE|                5       Singlet         -ABBA-                       5.5648
 BSE|                6       Singlet         -ABBA-                       5.6090
 BSE|                7       Singlet         -ABBA-                       5.7604
 BSE|                8       Singlet         -ABBA-                       5.9051
 BSE|                9       Singlet         -ABBA-                       6.1205
 BSE|               10       Singlet         -ABBA-                       6.2159
 BSE|               11       Singlet         -ABBA-                       6.3238
 BSE|               12       Singlet         -ABBA-                       6.3412
 BSE|               13       Singlet         -ABBA-                       6.4007
 BSE|               14       Singlet         -ABBA-                       6.5332
 BSE|               15       Singlet         -ABBA-                       6.6615
 BSE|               16       Singlet         -ABBA-                       6.6924
 BSE|               17       Singlet         -ABBA-                       6.7218
 BSE|               18       Singlet         -ABBA-                       6.7778
 BSE|               19       Singlet         -ABBA-                       6.7949
 BSE|               20       Singlet         -ABBA-                       6.8134
 BSE|               21       Singlet         -ABBA-                       6.8426
 BSE|               22       Singlet         -ABBA-                       6.8487
 BSE|               23       Singlet         -ABBA-                       6.8951
 BSE|               24       Singlet         -ABBA-                       6.9807
 BSE|               25       Singlet         -ABBA-                       7.1804
 BSE|
 BSE| Single-particle transitions are built up by (de-)excitations,
 BSE| which we denote by
 BSE|
 BSE|              =>        for excitations, i.e. entries of X_ia^n,
 BSE|              <=        for deexcitations, i.e. entries of Y_ia^n.
 BSE|
 BSE| The following single-particle transitions have significant contributions,
 BSE| i.e. |X_ia^n| > 0.100 or |Y_ia^n| > 0.100, respectively :
